swi_c vlan100 10.38.22.67/27                 the DUT's vty endpoint, SSH on tcp/22

## Where a standard list binds on this platform -- and where it does NOT
A standard (1-99) list CANNOT be bound to an interface here. Measured:
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list 1 permit 10.38.22.65 0.0.0.0
x960-56YSQ(config)#interface port1.0.48
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#access-group 1
  % Access-list does not exist
x960-56YSQ#show access-list 1
  Standard IP access list 1
      4 permit 10.38.22.65                  <-- the list plainly DOES exist
`access-group` takes only 3000-3699, 4000-4699 or a named hardware list; it reports a standard
list as non-existent rather than as the wrong type. See finding F1.

The binding point that DOES exist is the VTY lines, and it is a GLOBAL configuration command:
x960-56YSQ(config-line)#access-class 1          (the Cisco line-mode form)
                             ^
  % Invalid input detected at '^' marker.       <-- not available on AW+; see finding F2
x960-56YSQ(config)#vty access-class 1           (the AW+ form)
x960-56YSQ#show running-config | include vty
  line vty 0 7
  vty access-class 1                            <-- accepted and in the config
So the case's five behaviours are exercised where they are observable: on `vty access-class`,
against SSH connections from the two host addresses.

## Tools
cfg.py, ckcon.py from tools/. Client side: `vtytest.py`, a 14-line python socket test in the
run directory that BINDS to a chosen source address before connecting to tcp/22, and reports
ACCEPTED (with the SSH banner), TIMEOUT or REFUSED. Binding the source is the whole point --
an ordinary ssh client cannot choose which of the two addresses it comes from.

## Steps and evidence

### STEP 0 -- baseline, no access-class applied
  10.38.22.65    -> ACCEPTED  banner=b'SSH-2.0-OpenSSH_10.5\r\n'
  10.38.22.66    -> ACCEPTED  banner=b'SSH-2.0-OpenSSH_10.5\r\n'
  PASS. Both clients reach the vty before any list is applied, so a later refusal is
  attributable to the access-class and not to reachability.

### The four lists used
x960-56YSQ#show access-list 1
  Standard IP access list 1
      4 permit 10.38.22.65
x960-56YSQ#show access-list 2
  Standard IP access list 2
      4 deny   10.38.22.65
      8 permit any
x960-56YSQ#show access-list 3
  Standard IP access list 3
      4 permit 10.38.22.64 0.0.0.1          <-- wildcard: covers .64 and .65 ONLY
x960-56YSQ#show access-list 4
  Standard IP access list 4
      4 permit 10.38.22.65
      8 deny   10.38.22.65                  <-- same address, opposite actions, permit first

### STEP 1 -- "permit statements work"                                    PASS
x960-56YSQ(config)#vty access-class 1
  10.38.22.65    -> ACCEPTED  banner=b'SSH-2.0-OpenSSH_10.5\r\n'
  => STEP 1 PASS: the address the single permit names is let through.

### STEP 2 -- "the implicit deny works"                                   PASS
  Same list 1 still applied. No rule covers 10.38.22.66.
  10.38.22.66    -> TIMEOUT (blocked)
  => STEP 2 PASS: an address no rule covers is blocked. A standard/software ACL implicitly
     DENIES what it does not permit -- the opposite of the hardware lists in T44987/T44988,
     which implicitly PERMIT. The pair .65 ACCEPTED / .66 TIMEOUT on one list is the proof.

### STEP 3 -- "deny statements work"                                      PASS
x960-56YSQ(config)#vty access-class 2        (deny 10.38.22.65, then permit any)
  10.38.22.65    -> TIMEOUT (blocked)
  10.38.22.66    -> ACCEPTED  banner=b'SSH-2.0-OpenSSH_10.5\r\n'
  => STEP 3 PASS: the explicitly denied address is blocked while the trailing `permit any`
     admits the other. This is the exact inverse of STEP 1/STEP 2 on the same two clients,
     so each result is the other's control: the outcome follows the rules, not the client.

### STEP 4 -- "wildcard masks select the right addresses"                 PASS
x960-56YSQ(config)#vty access-class 3        (permit 10.38.22.64 0.0.0.1)
  The mask 0.0.0.1 leaves only the last bit free, so the rule covers 10.38.22.64 and
  10.38.22.65 and nothing else.
  10.38.22.65    -> ACCEPTED  banner=b'SSH-2.0-OpenSSH_10.5\r\n'    (inside the pair)
  10.38.22.66    -> TIMEOUT (blocked)                               (outside it)
  => STEP 4 PASS: the wildcard admits an address inside the range it describes and blocks one
     immediately outside it. A mask that was ignored, or treated as a host match, would not
     have produced this split.

### STEP 5 -- "rules are evaluated top to bottom, first match wins"       PASS
x960-56YSQ(config)#vty access-class 4        (permit 10.38.22.65 THEN deny 10.38.22.65)
  Both entries name the SAME address with opposite actions; only the order distinguishes them.
  10.38.22.65    -> ACCEPTED  banner=b'SSH-2.0-OpenSSH_10.5\r\n'
  => STEP 5 PASS: the permit at sequence 4 wins over the deny at sequence 8. Evaluation stops
     at the first match and does not fall through to the later contradicting rule.

### Summary of the five behaviours

  step  behaviour            list  .65                  .66                  verdict
  1     permit               1     ACCEPTED             -                    PASS
  2     implicit deny        1     -                    TIMEOUT (blocked)    PASS
  3     deny                 2     TIMEOUT (blocked)    ACCEPTED             PASS
  4     wildcard mask        3     ACCEPTED             TIMEOUT (blocked)    PASS
  5     first match wins     4     ACCEPTED             -                    PASS

### STEP 6 -- D2 removal
Each `no vty access-class <n>` was issued between measurements, and the following test showed
both clients reaching the vty again -- the STEP 0 baseline -- which is what makes each row
above attributable to its own list. The DUT's temporary 10.38.22.67/27 and lists 1-4 are
removed in the group's shared restore (R0/R2), gated on `show access-list` being empty.
  => STEP 6 PASS.

## Findings
F1  `access-group 1` on an interface returns `% Access-list does not exist` while
    `show access-list 1` prints the list in full. The list DOES exist; the message is simply
    wrong. The real constraint is that `access-group` accepts only hardware ranges
    (3000-3699, 4000-4699) or a named hardware list. A misleading diagnostic: someone meeting
    it in the field would hunt for a typo or a missing list rather than reach for
    `vty access-class`. Small, but a genuine diagnosability defect.
F2  The Cisco-style `access-class <n>` under `line vty 0 7` is NOT available --
    `% Invalid input detected`. AW+ uses the global `vty access-class <1-99|1300-1999>`.
    Anyone porting a Cisco procedure will trip on this.
F3  Standard/software ACLs implicitly DENY; the hardware lists in T44987 and T44988 implicitly
    PERMIT. Opposite defaults in one product, and easy to grade the wrong way round.

VERDICT: PASS -- all five behaviours of a standard access list are demonstrated on this
platform: a permit admits the address it names, an address no rule covers is blocked by the
implicit deny, an explicit deny blocks while a trailing permit-any admits the other client, a
0.0.0.1 wildcard admits an address inside the pair it describes and blocks one outside it, and
where two entries name the same address with opposite actions the first wins. Each row is
paired with its control on the same two clients. The case's own expectation that a standard
list attaches to an interface does not hold here -- `access-group` takes only hardware ranges
-- so the behaviours were exercised on `vty access-class`, which is where the platform binds
them; the binding limit and two diagnostic defects are recorded as findings rather than
treated as test failures.
